1
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:08,170
Hi. I'm Rowena, a
Senior Program Manager

2
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:09,860
in the Systems Productivity and

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:12,155
Reporting Team in Google Cloud.

4
00:00:12,155 --> 00:00:13,355
As a project manager,

5
00:00:13,355 --> 00:00:14,780
you'll find that
you're not actually

6
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:17,965
the direct manager of
people in their roles.

7
00:00:17,965 --> 00:00:21,290
The key soft skill
to have here is

8
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,365
around influence; influencing
without authority.

9
00:00:25,365 --> 00:00:27,250
It's the ability to be able to

10
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,770
communicate with people
and make sure that

11
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:32,020
they understand your
goals and objectives and

12
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:35,045
take them along on a shared
vision and shared journey.

13
00:00:35,045 --> 00:00:37,720
When I first became
a project manager,

14
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:40,870
one of the mistakes that I
often made was trying to do

15
00:00:40,870 --> 00:00:44,290
everything for myself
and not asking for help.

16
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:46,810
Over the course of the
last couple of years,

17
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:49,060
I've really learned how
to delegate and work

18
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,700
with others as part
of a team to make

19
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:54,940
sure that I'm effectively
managing a project as

20
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:57,490
a project manager
regardless of whether I

21
00:00:57,490 --> 00:01:00,850
have management or authority
over the project team,

22
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:02,590
and allowing those SMEs to

23
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:04,730
really do what they're
really good at.

24
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:09,540
Delegating for me in my
day-to-day role looks like this:

25
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:12,970
I try to find subject matter
experts or people that

26
00:01:12,970 --> 00:01:15,310
really enjoy doing a
specific something

27
00:01:15,310 --> 00:01:17,040
that may not actually
be their role.

28
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:19,210
One of the best
pieces of advice I

29
00:01:19,210 --> 00:01:21,370
ever got from a
mentor was around

30
00:01:21,370 --> 00:01:24,040
finding people in the
organization that

31
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:27,265
enjoy doing something and
delegating your work to them.

32
00:01:27,265 --> 00:01:29,740
That way you can focus
on overall project

33
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:32,170
or program management
without having to get

34
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:33,550
bogged down in the weeds by

35
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:35,710
certain details that
actually you might not enjoy

36
00:01:35,710 --> 00:01:39,385
doing or someone else
really enjoys doing.

37
00:01:39,385 --> 00:01:42,190
Psychological safety
is really important in

38
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:43,570
teams because it brings

39
00:01:43,570 --> 00:01:46,140
diversity of thought
to the table.

40
00:01:46,140 --> 00:01:49,510
One thing that I'm really
working on is making sure

41
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:52,915
that we practice
inclusive meetings.

42
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:54,430
An example of that could

43
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:56,650
be in a meeting if
you notice that

44
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:58,630
one or two participants

45
00:01:58,630 --> 00:02:01,330
are usually quiet
or don't speak up,

46
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:03,370
try to encourage
them to be a part

47
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:05,550
of the conversation. Even better,

48
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:07,300
have a coffee with
them before and

49
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,685
ask them what they think
of the upcoming topic.

50
00:02:09,685 --> 00:02:12,810
Encourage them to bring
those ideas to the table.

51
00:02:12,810 --> 00:02:16,775
Having diversity of thought
in the room is so important.

52
00:02:16,775 --> 00:02:18,220
Otherwise, you're
going to end up

53
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:20,230
with one person in the room

54
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:22,210
constantly just
bringing their thoughts

55
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:23,680
and ideas to the table.

56
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,565
That's not very diverse.

57
00:02:25,565 --> 00:02:27,879
One thing with
psychological safety

58
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:34,005
is this partnership with
mentorship and sponsorship.

59
00:02:34,005 --> 00:02:38,120
To delegate, I make sure
that I sponsor people.

60
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:40,640
I make sure that I showcase

61
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:44,120
their best quality work across
the whole project team.

62
00:02:44,120 --> 00:02:46,425
It's really important
for morale.

63
00:02:46,425 --> 00:02:48,705
On the other side
with mentorship,

64
00:02:48,705 --> 00:02:50,300
if there's a junior team member

65
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:51,770
on a team that
maybe doesn't have

66
00:02:51,770 --> 00:02:55,430
the experience or isn't
as confident speaking up,

67
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:57,350
I usually delegate
smaller tasks to

68
00:02:57,350 --> 00:02:59,615
them to build up
their confidence or

69
00:02:59,615 --> 00:03:02,090
delegate something that they
might actually consider to

70
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:04,775
be fun that's not in their
normal day-to-day work.

71
00:03:04,775 --> 00:03:07,220
Finding those unique balances is

72
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:10,680
the best way to delegate
and prioritize.
